 ## Overview
-MS-NeRF addresses the failure of standard single-space NeRF methods to render reflective and refractive objects, which typically result in blurry or distorted images due to violated multi-view consistency in Euclidean space [S1]. It differs from its siblings by representing the scene as a group of feature fields in parallel sub-spaces rather than a single radiance field, allowing virtual images to be placed in sub-spaces visible only from specific viewpoints [S1]. This approach serves as a general enhancement module for existing NeRF backbones (MLP-based or grid-based) with minimal computational overhead [S1].
+MS-NeRF addresses the failure of standard single-space Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to render reflective and refractive objects, which often results in blurry or distorted images due to violated multi-view consistency [S1, S2]. It represents the scene using a group of feature fields in parallel sub-spaces instead of a single radiance field in Euclidean space, allowing virtual images to be placed in sub-spaces visible only from certain views [S1]. Unlike single-space NeRF, which often requires manual labeling of reflective surfaces to avoid sub-optimal convergence, MS-NeRF automatically handles mirror-like objects without such labeling [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-MS-NeRF replaces the standard output layer of a backbone NeRF network with a multi-space module that outputs multiple densities and feature vectors per ray sample, corresponding to different sub-spaces [S1].
+MS-NeRF modifies the output layer of a backbone NeRF method (e.g., NeRF, Mip-NeRF 360, TensoRF) by replacing the standard single-output head with a multi-space module [S1]. This module leverages the Feature Fields principle, where the network predicts arbitrary per-point feature signals in addition to color and density [Parent].
 
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** Ray $r$, backbone network parameters, hyperparameters $K$ (number of sub-spaces) and $d$ (feature dimension).
-*   **Output:** Final rendered color $\hat{C}(r)$.
